Operating cash reflects core activities; investing acquires or disposes of investments and long-lived assets; financing changes funding claims.|Cash from sales ₹20, equipment spending ₹8, and new borrowing ₹4 net to ₹16 inflow.|Equipment purchase is generally classified here as…?|Investing|Sales revenue|Equity income
Direct CFO shows major cash receipts and payments; indirect CFO reconciles a profit measure with cash.|Profit ₹16 + depreciation ₹5 − working-capital increase ₹10 gives ₹11 simplified CFO.|Correct direct and indirect approaches should produce…?|The same CFO under consistent classification|Different closing cash by design|Only financing cash
Profit-to-cash reconciliation adjusts non-cash charges, accrual timing, and items classified elsewhere.|Profit ₹20 + depreciation ₹4 − receivable increase ₹7 gives ₹17 simplified CFO.|Depreciation add-back is a new receipt?|No|Yes|Only for machines
Non-cash expenses and income affect profit without a matching current-period cash flow. Reverse them appropriately in indirect reconciliation.|Subtract an asset-disposal gain from operating profit reconciliation and show sale proceeds in investing.|Why subtract the disposal gain from the reconciliation?|Avoid treating investing gain as operating cash|Erase the sale completely|Create another expense
An increase in operating assets generally absorbs cash; an increase in operating liabilities generally releases cash, with other effects held constant.|Receivables +₹5 and payables +₹2 create a net ₹3 operating working-capital use.|Net cash effect is…?|−₹3|+₹7|+₹3
Capex is cash spent on qualifying assets; disposal proceeds are the full cash received, distinct from accounting gain.|Sell PPE carrying ₹8 for ₹10: investing inflow ₹10 and gain ₹2.|Investing proceeds equal…?|₹10|₹2|₹8
Acquisitions and investments move cash into ownership claims. Cash acquired and non-cash consideration need separate treatment.|Pay ₹100 for a company containing ₹20 cash: simplified net acquisition outflow ₹80.|A share-for-share acquisition necessarily pays cash?|No|Yes|Only for subsidiaries
Debt issuance brings financing cash; principal repayment uses it. Net borrowing is issuance less repayment.|Borrow ₹10 and repay ₹6: net borrowing inflow ₹4.|Interest expense is identical to principal repayment?|No|Yes|Only on maturity
Equity issuance funds owners' claims; buybacks and dividends distribute value. Classification depends on framework and period.|Issue equity ₹10 and distribute ₹2: simplified net owner funding ₹8.|A dividend is operating revenue?|No|Yes|Only if reinvested
Interest and dividend cash-flow classifications vary by framework, entity activity, and effective reporting period.|Two comparable businesses may classify interest differently; restate consistently before comparing CFO.|A CFO difference necessarily proves different economics?|No|Yes|Only for debt-free firms
Exchange-rate changes alter translated foreign-currency cash balances without an ordinary cash transaction.|$100 cash translated at ₹80 becomes ₹8,500 at ₹85: ₹500 translation increase.|That ₹500 is necessarily customer cash collection?|No|Yes|Only if dollars were retained
Cash reconciliation connects opening and closing qualifying balances, including relevant restriction and exchange effects.|₹15 opening + ₹11 CFO − ₹8 capex + ₹4 borrowing − ₹2 dividend = ₹20 closing.|Closing cash is…?|₹20|₹40|₹15
Free cash flow has several definitions; FCFF serves all capital providers, while FCFE serves equity after financing flows.|NOPAT ₹20 + D&A ₹5 − capex ₹8 − ΔOWC ₹10 gives FCFF ₹7.|FCFF should be discounted using…?|A consistent WACC|Only cost of equity|The gross margin
Each transaction can affect multiple statements, often at different times. Trace entries before interpreting cash effects.|A credit sale creates revenue and receivables; later collection increases cash and reduces receivables.|Collection repeats the income-statement sale?|No|Yes|Only next year
Profitable firms can exhaust cash through collection delays, inventory, capex, and debt service.|Profit ₹20 with ₹30 working-capital investment can imply negative cash generation.|Which action most directly releases receivables cash?|Collect existing customer balances|Recognise the same revenue again|Increase the allowance
Cash-rich firms can report losses from non-cash charges or retain funds raised earlier. Cash stock and period profit answer different questions.|A ₹50 impairment lowers profit without a simultaneous ₹50 payment.|A loss necessarily means cash fell by the same amount?|No|Yes|Only for asset impairments
